The '''1978 Austria v West Germany match''' (known in Austria as ''Das Wunder von Córdoba'' (''The Miracle of Córdoba'') and in Germany as ''Die Schmach von Córdoba'' (''The Disgrace of Córdoba'')<ref name="soc" />), describes the game of [[association football|football]] between [[Austria national football team|Austria]] and defending World Champions [[Germany national football team|West Germany]] that occurred on 21 June 1978 at the conclusion of the second round of the [[1978 FIFA World Cup]], in the city of [[Córdoba, Argentina|Córdoba]].<ref name="soc">{{Cite web|url=http://bundesligafanatic.com/germany-v-austria-1978-unravelling-the-shame-of-cordoba/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120913044802/http://bundesligafanatic.com/germany-v-austria-1978-unravelling-the-shame-of-cordoba/|url-status=usurped|archive-date=13 September 2012|title = Bundesliga News – all the German football news, live scores and stats}}</ref>

The game is fondly remembered by fans of the Austria national team for being the first time in 47 years that Austria had defeated a team from either the [[West Germany|Western]] or [[East Germany|Eastern]] side of the then-partitioned Germany.

==The build-up==
In the first round, [[Austria national football team|Austria]] had surprisingly won their group, ahead of [[Brazil national football team|Brazil]] even though they had lost to them. A rather lacklustre [[Germany national football team|West Germany]] had only finished second in their group, following two scoreless draws. While the West German team still contained a number of players who had contributed to winning the [[1974 World Cup]], others had retired from the national team, being dissatisfied with the old-fashioned attitude of [[German Football Association|German Football Association (DFB)]] leaders and coach [[Helmut Schön]], who at age 62 was about to retire after the tournament.

In the second round, the Austrians lost their first two games, against the [[Netherlands national football team|Netherlands]] (1–5) and [[Italy national football team|Italy]] (0–1), which meant that they were already eliminated from contention before the last game. The West Germans had managed another two draws against the Dutch (opponents in the 1974 final) and the Italians (opponents in the 1970 semi-final, the so-called [[Game of the Century (soccer)|Game of the Century]]).

The final group games of the second round gave West Germany and Italy the opportunity to determine directly who would advance to the Final. If their simultaneous games (both at 13:45 local time) ended in a draw (there was no [[Overtime (sports)#Knock-out contests (including professional competition)|extra-time]] or [[Penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalty shoot-out]], as it was a group stage game), then the Netherlands would progress to the final and Italy would qualify for the match for third place.  A draw between the Dutch and the Italians combined with a decisive five goal victory by West Germany over Austria would give the West Germans the advantage over the Dutch, and a berth in the Final. If the Netherlands–Italy game had a winner, West Germany could still qualify for the match for third place by beating Austria, or in case of an Italian loss (or Netherlands loss by at least four goals), even with a draw.

