==Aftermath==
West German fans were dissatisfied with the performance and attitude of their 1978 team, contrasted with the Austrians, who fought fiercely with their neighbours in a game that had no significance to Austria in terms of further progression in the tournament. Finger's phrase "I wer' narrisch!" ("I'm going crazy!") became the slogan of several marketing and advertising campaigns.<ref>{{cite news|last=Hochwarter |first=Thomas |title=Edi ´I wer´ narrisch!´ Finger died 20 years ago |url=http://austriantimes.at/news/Sports/2009-04-10/12455/Edi%20%C2%B4I%20wer%C2%B4%20narrisch!%C2%B4%20Finger%20died%2020%20years%20ago |accessdate=13 October 2013 |newspaper=Austrian Times |date=10 April 2009 |url-status=dead |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20141208150800/http://austriantimes.at/news/Sports/2009-04-10/12455/Edi%20%C2%B4I%20wer%C2%B4%20narrisch%21%C2%B4%20Finger%20died%2020%20years%20ago |archivedate=8 December 2014 }}</ref>

With a new coach, [[Jupp Derwall]], the West Germans went on to win the [[UEFA Euro 1980|1980 European championship]].<ref>{{cite web|title=1980 UEFA European Championship|url=http://www.uefa.com/uefaeuro/season=1980/index.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111116162423/http://www.uefa.com/uefaeuro/season=1980/index.html|url-status=dead|archive-date=16 November 2011|publisher=[[UEFA]]|access-date=13 October 2013}}</ref> Then in 1982, at the [[1982 FIFA World Cup|next World Cup in Spain]], West Germany and Austria met again, in a final group-stage game called the [[Disgrace of Gijón]] due to both teams seemingly cooperating in order to advance at the cost of a third team, Algeria. This time, the West Germans had to win to avoid elimination from the tournament, but doing so by only one or two goals would not harm the Austrians and send both teams through to the next round at the expense of [[Algeria national football team|Algeria]], who had beaten West Germany 2–1 in the first game of the group. Hence, after West Germany scored early, the two teams slowed down in the second half, and towards the end, kicked the ball around aimlessly, in stark contrast to 1978, and to worldwide howls of derision. The game was subsequently dubbed the "Nichtangriffspakt von Gijón" (Non-aggression Pact of Gijón).<ref>{{cite web|title=World Cup Tales: The Shame Of Gijon, 1982|url=http://twohundredpercent.net/?p=6088|publisher=200%|accessdate=13 October 2013|archive-date=19 September 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150919022725/http://twohundredpercent.net/?p=6088|url-status=dead}}</ref>

Since that match in 1982, as of 2025, Germany has played in five World Cup finals, winning two, whilst Austria has only qualified for two World Cups ([[1990 FIFA World Cup|1990]] and [[1998 FIFA World Cup|1998]]), going out in the group stage both times. Austria also played the by now re-unified [[German reunification|Germany]] at [[UEFA Euro 2008 Group B]], co-hosted between Austria and [[Switzerland national football team|Switzerland]]. On this occasion, Germany defeated Austria 1–0 in their capital Vienna to qualify for the next round and knock the co-hosts out of the tournament.<ref>{{cite web|title=Austria 0 Germany 1|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuro/match/300700--austria-vs-germany/|publisher=[[UEFA]]|access-date=13 October 2013}}</ref>

On 16 May 2008, exactly one month before the Euro 2008 group match between Austria and Germany, the Swiss performance artist {{Interlanguage link|Massimo Furlan|fr}} staged a one-man re-enactment of the 1978 World Cup match between the sides in front of 2,500 spectators at [[Gerhard Hanappi Stadium|Rapid Vienna's stadium]]. Furlan imitated Hans Krankl's match performance and goal celebrations, with {{Interlanguage link|Edi Finger Jr.|de|3=Edi Finger junior}} reproducing his father's famous radio commentary of the match.<ref>{{cite news|last=Woebking|first=Angela|title=The Cordoba Miracle; actor Massimo Furlan reenacted Hans Krankl's heroic 1978 winning goal for Austria to live commentary By Eddy Finger Jr.|url=http://www.viennareview.net/on-the-town/city-life/the-cordoba-miracle|accessdate=13 October 2013|newspaper=[[The Vienna Review]]|date=1 June 2008|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141210150909/http://www.viennareview.net/on-the-town/city-life/the-cordoba-miracle|archive-date=10 December 2014|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.historisch.apa.at/cms/apa-historisch/dossier.html?dossierID=AHD_19780621_AHD0001 |title=APA - Austria Presse Agentur / 55-85 apa historisch ť zeitgeschichte online |publisher=Historisch.apa.at |date=20 December 2004 |accessdate=19 August 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110820175717/http://www.historisch.apa.at/cms/apa-historisch/dossier.html?dossierID=AHD_19780621_AHD0001 |archive-date=20 August 2011 |url-status=dead |df=dmy-all }}</ref>

